\
Each Unit consists of one common share at a price of $0.59, one-half (1/2) of one series "A" share purchase warrant at a price of $0.005 and one-half (1/2) of one series "B" share purchase warrant at a price of $0.005. One (1) whole series "A" share purchase warrant shall entitle its holder thereof to purchase one (1) additional common share of the share capital of the Company at $0.75 for a period of 12 months. One (1) whole series "B" share purchase warrant shall entitle its holder thereof to purchase one (1) additional common share of the share capital of the Company at $1.00 for a period of 18 months. \
\
The securities issued under the private placement will be subject to a four-month hold period expiring on March 30, 2006. \
\
The proceeds of the private placement will be used for the purchase of equipment for the production of advanced material and nanomaterial products and technologies as well as the hiring of additional human resources and as working capital. \
\
The Board of Directors of the Company has adopted a shareholders' rights plan (the "Rights Plan"). \
\
"By implementing the rights plan at this time, the Board of Directors have taken pro-active steps to ensure that the shareholders of the Company can avail themselves of it should an unsolicited offer be made for the Company. The purpose of the plan is to provide the Company and its Board of Directors and shareholders with sufficient time to properly consider any offers to acquire control of the Company," said Stephane Robert, President and Chief Executive Officer of the Company. \
\
Under existing securities legislation a take-over bid need only to remain open for 35 days; a period of time which the Board of Directors believe could be insufficient to fully evaluate an offer, make an informed recommendation to the shareholders of the Company and if considered appropriate pursue alternatives which could maximize value for the Company and its shareholders. Under the terms of the Company's Rights Plan, a "permitted bid" must be made by means of a take-over bid circular sent to all shareholders of the Company, remain open for acceptance for at least 60 days and require acceptance by at least 50% of the common shares held by shareholders not affiliated or associated with the Offeror. \
\
The Rights Plan takes effect immediately but is subject to the regulatory approval. Further, shareholders of the Company will be asked to approve the Rights Plan at a meeting of the shareholders of the Company to be held on December 21, 2005. If approved at this meeting, the initial term of the Rights Plan will expire at the close of the annual meeting of the shareholders of the Company in 2008. \
\
In the context of the plan, a flip-in event occurs when a person, acting alone or jointly in concert with another, acquires or offers to acquire more than 20% of the outstanding common shares, other than by way of a permitted bid, a competing permitted bid or in certain other limited circumstances. \
\
If the potential offerors do not comply with these conditions, each right (other than those held by the acquiring person) will permit the purchase of common shares with a market value of $200 on payment of the exercise price of $100 per right on the date of the flip-in event which is deemed to be effective at the close of business on the 10th trading day following the announcement of the offer. \
\
Until the occurrence of a flip-in event, the rights will trade together with the common shares and will be represented by the unit certificates. Upon the occurrence of a flip-in event, the rights will be evidenced by rights certificates and will trade separately from the units. The rights do not carry any other rights attaching to the units such as voting or distribution rights. \
\
The Company is not adopting the Rights Plan in response to any specific proposal to acquire control of the Company. The Rights Plan is similar to plans adopted by other Canadian companies and approved by their shareholders. \
