ROCHESTER, N.Y. - (BUSINESS WIRE) - Nov. 8, 2005 - Biophan Technologies, Inc. (OTCBB: BIPH; FWB: BTN), a developer of next-generation medical technology, announced that Company researchers and research partners were invited to present scientific papers at a high-profile meeting of leading federal healthcare policymakers and industry professionals. The special meeting on MRI safety was sponsored by the International Society for Magnetic Resonance in Medicine (ISMRM), and was held Nov. 5-6 in McLean, Virginia. \
\
"The field of Magnetic Resonance Imaging ('MRI') continues to undergo rapid changes including the introduction of more powerful MRI systems and new applications of the technology," said Jeffrey L. Helfer, Biophan's Vice President of Engineering. "The combination of an increasing number of patients receiving implanted medical devices and safety issues associated with the use of these devices in the MRI environment has caused over 200,000 patients per year to be denied MRI exams. As a result, medical device manufacturers are searching for technologies they can purchase or license to enable their devices to be used safely with MRI, which is creating growing interest in Biophan's technologies." \
\
Present at the ISMRM meeting were scientists, device manufacturers, FDA officials, physicians, as well as MRI nurses and technologists. Three presentations were made by Biophan and its affiliates at the ISMRM meeting. The titles are listed below: \
\
-- "Influence of Pacemaker Lead Design Geometry and Placement Configuration on MRI-induced Heating and Implanted Leads." Presenter: Jeffrey L. Helfer, Biophan Vice President, Engineering. \
\
-- "Inductively Coupled MR Visualization of Intraluminal Substrates of Vascular Implants, Principles and Safety Aspects." Presenter: Andreas Melzer, Chief Technology Officer, Biophan-Europe. \
\
-- "A Basic Investigation of Heating Effects on Total Hip Prosthesis in Combination with a Simulated Skin Contact of the Inner Thighs During Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) with a 1.5 Tesla MR System." Author: Gregor Schaefers, Managing Director of MR:comp GmbH, a company affiliated with Biophan through its Biophan-Europe division. \
\
"At this meeting, we presented new technologies for expanding the use of MRI," Mr. Helfer continued. "Heating of pacemaker and other leads can vary widely from one lead to another, as well as from one manufacturer to another, under identical conditions. This variability is further compounded by conditions which include: lead configuration (length and design), MRI machine type and settings, position of the patient in the coil, geometry of the lead within the patient, etc. As a result, safety standards must address the combination of all of these worst case conditions. Biophan solutions, tested under worst case conditions, were consistently able to reduce heating to acceptable levels. \
\
Mr. Helfer's presentation, made at the ISMRM meeting, can be viewed on the Biophan website at http://www.biophan.com/presentations.php . Biophan expects to post the other presentations on its website in the near future. \
\
About ISMRM \
\
The International Society for Magnetic Resonance in Medicine is a nonprofit professional association devoted to furthering the development and application of magnetic resonance techniques in medicine and biology. The Society holds annual scientific meetings and sponsors other major educational and scientific workshops. The current workshop is expected to be attended by officials of the FDA, major academic institutions, and MRI technologists, physicists, physiologists, bioengineers, researchers, system and accessory suppliers, and others, according to its organizers. For more information, please visit http://www.ismrm.org . \
\
About Biophan Technologies \
\
Biophan develops and markets cutting-edge technologies designed to make biomedical devices safe and image compatible with the magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) environment. The Company develops technologies that enable implanted medical systems such as pacemakers, interventional surgical devices such as catheters and guidewires, and devices such as stents to be safely and effectively imaged under MRI. Biophan is developing these same technologies to provide new MRI contrast agents. Other applications include drug delivery and power systems derived from body heat. Four Biophan technologies include advances in nanotechnology and thin film coatings. Committed to growth through innovation and developmental leadership, Biophan and its licensors now hold a total of 156 U.S. patents, licenses, or applications. This total includes 42 issued U.S. patents, 8 recently-allowed applications that will issue as patents in the near future, and 106 pending applications at various stages of examination at the U.S. Patent and Trademark Office, plus international applications. The patents cover areas including nanotechnology (nanomagnetic particle coatings), medical device designs, radio frequency filters, polymer composites, thermoelectric materials for batteries generating power from body heat and optical catheter technologies. Biophan has joint development and licensing agreements with Boston Scientific and NASA's Ames Center for Nanotechnology. Biophan's goal is to make all biomedical devices capable of safely and successfully working with MRI, and delivering other technologies which will improve quality of life. Biophan is also listed on the Frankfurt Stock Exchange under the ticker symbol "BTN." For more information, please visit www.biophan.com . \
