\
A growing number of companies are raising the bar by using intellectual property as a strategic lever to gain competitive advantage, according to a new edition of the scorecard that tracks corporate innovation. \
\
"Our analysis distinguishes between patent quantity and patent quality, which is the real key to innovation," said Jim Finnegan, vice president of business development for ipIQ, which released an advance copy of the 2006 Patent Scorecard(TM) today. "The same analysis demonstrates that U.S. companies have maintained their technological leadership as intellectual property and patenting increase in prominence as drivers of the global economy." \
\
The Patent Scorecard analyzes several decades of patent data collected on over 50,000 companies across 14 industries. Significant trends and company movements within these industries include: \
\
-- Despite a decline in patent activity, two biotechnology companies, Human Genome Sciences and Large Scale Biology, moved from the 12th to 5th position and from the 19th to 8th position respectively, on the basis of improved invention quality. \
\
-- Telecommunications continued to maintain a high level of patenting. Within the telecommunications industry, Motorola has filed fewer patents but has significantly increased patent quality. Among the companies making significant moves, ipIQ identified Finisar as an industry up-and-comer, moving sharply in the standings from the 25th position last year to the 10th position this year. \
\
-- In the medical devices industry, nanotechnology is emerging to become the fastest-growing industry driver in history. Among the leaders in the field are Intuitive Surgical, which maintained the 8th position while Gyrus Group, a strong up-and-comer, rose from the 40th position last year to the 24th in the current ranking. \
\
-- In pharmaceuticals, most of the top companies continue to use acquisitions to focus their strategy as they reduce the rate of their patent filings. Teva Pharmaceuticals climbed from the 28th to 22nd position and could continue upward with its pending acquisition of Ivax Corporation. \
\
-- Semiconductor companies continue to be granted more patents than any other industry, and the sector leaders remained fairly consistent. Applied Materials broke into the top ten at the 7th position with strong links to core science and is emerging as a leader providing the building blocks for other innovations. (For additional findings, see fact sheet below, "Leaders by Industry.") \
\
Standings in the advance edition of the ipIQ Patent Scorecard are based on a comparison of data for the first three quarters of 2005, extrapolated to the full year, and on ipIQ's proprietary analytical toolset which assess innovation on four core criteria - technology strength, current impact index, science linkage, and technology cycle time. A full-year Patent Scorecard will be released in the first quarter of 2006. (For additional explanation, see fact sheet below, "Explanation of Patent Measures.") \
\
Comparing Technological Strength Internationally \
\
ipIQ's analysis showed that technological innovation and successful economic development are linked as closely on a national level as they are on the corporate level. In recent decades there has been significant growth in the number of patents filed by countries other than the U.S., beginning with Japan in the 1980s, Taiwan and South Korea in the 1990s, and more recently by China and India. Additionally, it is clear that the governments of both China and India have made intellectual property a priority to boost economic growth. \
\
"While the Patent Scorecard's corresponding benchmark study, the International Patent Standings, confirms these trends in China and India, it is important to note this patenting is largely sourced from foreign companies," said Finnegan. "While it will take some time for China and India to achieve the levels of corporate innovation tracked by the Patent Scorecard, their pace of growth cannot be ignored." \
\
"As trends in international patenting continue to develop, ipIQ's Patent Scorecard will monitor the degree of innovation, influence, and patent quality to identify changes in the competitive balance at the corporate, industry, and country levels," he said. \
\
ipIQ's compilation of the world's Technology Top 10 shows Asian companies occupying six positions. However, the four U.S. companies on the list have a clear advantage in terms of the quality of their patented technology. (For additional explanation, see fact sheet below, "The Technology Top 10.") \
\
Patent Analysis Growing in Importance \
\
ipIQ's analysis is a major advance compared to other patent portfolio valuation approaches, which rely on the quantity of patents awarded or a corporation's research and development budget, Finnegan explained. \
\
"ipIQ's mathematically-based analysis differs significantly from other methods to determine the value of a company's patents or technology strength relative to its competitors. Other methods include employing experts who subjectively assign patent value based on their individual knowledge of a given field, or simple citation analysis," he said. \
\
"What has distinguished our work over the past twenty years is the ability to place an objective value on patent portfolios and rank companies on the strength of their technology using a series of proven indicators rather than mere numbers of patents or citation counts," said Finnegan. "Accurately valuing intellectual property also is necessary to satisfy new financial regulations contained in the Sarbanes-Oxley Act, for example." \
\
"In addition, as the financial community begins to treat intellectual property as an asset class that can be financially engineered similar to securities backed by mortgages and other debt, we will continue to provide the Patent Scorecard and our analytical tools for assessing the economic impact of technology within industries." \
