\
Biophan recently announced a Letter of Intent with MYOTECH, LLC to acquire marketing rights to MYOTECH's breakthrough Ventricular Assist Device (VAD). Under the agreement, Biophan will also oversee the development and regulatory approval of MYOTECH's MYO-VAD(TM) cardiac assist technology, as well as all future MYOTECH development programs. In addition, at closing Biophan will acquire a substantial minority interest with the right to acquire a controlling interest in MYOTECH. \
\
The MYO-VAD device can potentially assist in the medical treatment of countless patients in a worldwide market for cardiac devices that is projected to reach $28 billion, making it one of the fastest growing segments of the biomedical marketplace. The new video about MYO-VAD features Biophan CEO, Michael Weiner, and Jeff Helfer, Biophan Vice President of Engineering and President of Biophan's Cardiovascular Division. It is the first in a three-part series of videos about the MYO-VAD. \
\
"Recent medical studies have shown that ventricular assist devices are superior to medical management that center on drug therapies," Mr. Weiner notes in the video. "But unfortunately, current VADs are associated with serious complications, including high rates of often fatal clotting, stroke, bleeding, and infection. While the therapeutic benefits of VADs are exciting, the complications associated with the devices available for use today are still completely unacceptable." \
\
Some leading medical device companies have already expressed initial interest in the MYO-VAD device. It is designed to assist the best blood pump known to man - the heart - to maintain healthy blood flow during heart failure. Moreover, the device is less invasive and therefore could be safer, simpler and more affordable than other devices on the market. Previous versions of Direct Mechanical Ventricular Actuation (DMVA) technology have been implanted experimentally in 700 animals, and successfully in five patients - helping to save their lives, thus demonstrating the effectiveness of the DMVA technology upon which the MYO-VAD design is based. \
\
