WOODBURY, N.Y. - (BUSINESS WIRE) - May 26, 2005 - Veeco Instruments Inc. (Nasdaq: VECO), a world leader in atomic force microscopy (AFM) technology, today announced that it has signed a joint development program (JDP) with CEA Leti, one of the largest applied research electronics laboratories in Europe, and Team Nanotec, a pioneer in the fabrication of AFM probe tips. Under the terms of the two-year agreement, Veeco, Leti and Team Nanotec will jointly develop tips to be used in Veeco's AFMs for the semiconductor market. \
\
The JDP will enable engineering teams from all three organizations to collaborate on development of next-generation tips, which will be critical as the semiconductor industry evolves below 70nm. Each organization has designated a team of personnel and will provide resources including tips, tools and samples with advanced design rules for this project. \
\
Team Nanotec develops, manufacturers and sells, among other products, AFM tips that are often used by Veeco's customers in conjunction with its systems. Team Nanotec specializes in small (less than 90nm), high aspect ratio tips. Leti has been a strong customer and collaborator with Veeco for several years, having used Veeco's AFMs in its 300mm clean room for 3-D imaging. \
\
Paul Clayton, Vice President, Business Unit Manager of Veeco's Automated AFM business commented, "As Veeco continues to penetrate leading fabs worldwide with our breadth of AFM solutions, we have a unique insight into our customers' technology roadmaps for critical dimension measurements. The goal of our collaboration is to develop production-worthy AFM tips and qualification standards that will meet our semiconductor customers' future technology requirements. In order to be ready to meet the 65nm, 45nm and sub-45nm metrology nodes, we require not only the advanced tip capability but also the structures with which to test them." \
\
Clayton continued, "Team Nanotec's experience with challenging tip applications will be critical to our future success and access to Leti's laboratories will give us the ability to test our tips in real world research and production environments. In this manner we can more closely align our production solutions with customer requirements." \
\
According to Johann Greschner, President of Team Nanotec, "We are pleased to be working with Veeco, the world leader in AFMs, on this technology development program." \
\
According to Olivier Demolliens, Director of NaNoTec Division at CEA Leti, "AFMs are becoming critical measurement tools in both advanced development laboratories and in production applications, and this joint development program will help us to remain ahead of the industry's technology roadmap." \
\
Veeco, with over ten years of experience selling AFMs to semiconductor fabs worldwide, now has an installed base of over 300 automated AFM tools. All Veeco automated AFM systems combine the lowest noise AFM capability and industry-leading speed, repeatability and accuracy. \
\
About CEA Leti \
\
The CEA plays a major role in research, development and innovation in three main fields: energy, information and healthcare technologies, and defence. Set up on 9 centres located all over France, the CEA benefits from a strong regional presence and solid partnerships with other research organisations, local authorities and universities. A laboratory of the CEA in Grenoble, the Laboratory of electronics and information technologies (Leti) is at the leading edge of European research on microelectronics and micro-nanotechnologies. It employs more than 900 people. With an average 200 patents filed per year and its twenty-eight start-ups created or in the course of creation, it is one of the major partners of the industrial world. Instigator of the MINATEC micro and nanotechnologies innovation centre project, the CEA Grenoble is also one of its main partners with the Grenoble National Polytechnic Institute (INPG) and the local authorities. Further information on Leti can be found at: http://www-leti.cea.fr/ . \
