PROVIDENCE, R.I. - (BUSINESS WIRE) - May 2, 2005 - Solaris Nanosciences Corporation announced today that it has closed on an investment made by the Slater Technology Fund, a venture development fund in Providence, RI, that focuses on cutting-edge technologies. Solaris Nanosciences is a new Rhode Island nanotechnology company developing and scaling up its technology to become a leading manufacturer of "nanoparticles" for use in next-generation products including cost effective solar energy panels, clearer video displays, and vision enhancement. \
\
"The investment made by the Slater Technology Fund is further validation that our company's technology is receiving an increasing amount of attention from the investment community," said Nabil Lawandy, Ph.D., President, Solaris Nanosciences. "Recent events, such as diminishing oil reserves coupled with increasing costs for extracting fossil fuels highlight the need for effective and scalable alternative forms of energy. Our technology is poised to dramatically change the energy landscape by harnessing solar energy in a more efficient and cost effective manner." \
\
As an early stage company, and spin-off from Spectra Systems, Solaris has completed the placement of a $150,000 convertible note with the Slater Technology Fund. These funds will be used to assist Solaris in its business development efforts. To date, Solaris has also received seed funding from Spectra Systems. \
\
The company has an active research and development program that is focusing on its nanoantenna technology and its application to significantly improve the performance of photovoltaic solar cells. A photovoltaic cell (PV cell) is a specialized semiconductor device that converts sunlight into electricity for home or industrial use. Photovoltaic cells are an integral part of solar-electric energy systems, which are becoming increasingly important as alternative sources of utility power. One of the major advantages of solar energy is the fact that it is non-polluting, requiring only real estate (and a reasonably sunny climate), in order to function. Another advantage is the fact that solar energy is unlimited. \
\
"Solaris' technology is one of the most innovative approaches I have seen for improving the efficiency and cost-effectiveness of solar energy," stated Ron Unterman, Ph.D., managing director of the Slater Technology Fund. "In addition, their core nanotechnology holds tremendous opportunity for other market sectors, including improved viewing angle for video displays, and enhanced vision for patients with macular degeneration. With its strong IP position and creative technology team, Solaris will continue to expand on its nanotechnology platform which underlies these and other potential products for both commercial and military markets." \
\
The science behind these applications is based on an effect observed by the Romans in the 4th century AD. By dissolving metals such as gold and silver into glass, Romans observed that new colors would appear in the glass that were completely different from the colors of the original bulk metals. In the 19th century, this effect was proven to derive from a type of optical absorption, called a plasmon, in metal particles whose dimensions are much smaller than the wavelength of light. Solaris' technology platform is based on exploiting the unique properties of plasmons. \
\
Solaris Nanosciences is creating high-volume, scalable, synthetic routes for the production of novel nanomaterial structures for enhanced solar cells and other applications. The company has successfully made core-shell structures for collaborative testing in solar cell applications at the Swiss Federal Institute where dye-sensitized solar cells were discovered and pioneered using conventional materials. In addition, they have also produced a number of nanomaterials for further surface modification and eventual use in vision and liquid crystal display applications. Efforts have also focused on the engineering of the enhancement effect through a series of key experiments, which when complete, will allow the company to begin the second phase of development where the materials will be characterized in terms of their enhanced light harvesting capabilities. The company has collaborative research relationships with institutional and corporate partners, including Brown University, the University of Rhode Island and Emitech. \
\
About Solaris Nanosciences Corporation \
\
The company is initially pursuing three major applications of its nanoantenna platform technology: advanced materials for photovoltaics, additives for liquid crystal displays and nanostructures for improving the response of photoreceptors in the eye and other biological systems. Solaris' initial application of its technology is the production of low-cost solar cells. For more information about Solaris Nanosciences and its technologies, visit www.solarisnano.com . \
\
About the Slater Technology Fund \
\
The Slater Technology Fund supports the development of emerging industries with potential for significant long-term growth within the State of Rhode Island. The Fund focuses resources on the support of entrepreneurs who have the vision, commitment and leadership to build substantial commercial enterprises. The Fund typically invests at the inception stage of a new venture, often based upon ideas and technologies originating in academic institutions and/or government research laboratories located within the region. In most cases, investments are premised upon the possibility of raising substantial follow-on financing, be it from venture capital investors, other private equity investors or strategic partners, the rationale being that such external funding will accelerate the process of generating a significant number of high value, high wage jobs over the intermediate to longer-term. For more information about the Slater Fund, visit www.slaterfund.com . \
