NEWTON, Mass. - (BUSINESS WIRE) - March 18, 2005 - MFIC Corporation ("MFIC" or the "Company") (OTCBB: MFIC) reported today its fiscal 2004 year-end and fourth quarter financial results. The presentation of results reflects the ongoing operations of the Company's sole operating subsidiary, Microfluidics Corporation ("Microfluidics"), and includes the discontinued operations of the divested Morehouse-COWLES Division. \
\
For the year ended December 31, 2004, revenues from the Company's continuing operations were $12,158,919, an increase of $1,699,288, or 16%, as compared with revenues from continuing operations of $10,459,631 for the corresponding period in 2003. For fiscal 2004 the Company posted net income from continuing operations of $655,752, or $0.07 per share before an income tax benefit in the amount of $450,000, as compared with $659,491 or $0.08 per share, for the comparable period in 2003. Realization of an income tax benefit of $450,000, or $0.04 per share in 2004 offset by a loss from discontinued operations of $231,380 (booked in the first quarter of 2004) resulted in net income per share, after all adjustments, in the amount of $874,372, or $0.09 per share. \
\
For the quarter ended December 31, 2004, the Company reported revenues from continuing operations of $3,661,705 as compared to $2,477,751 from continuing operations in the fourth quarter of 2003, representing an approximate 48% increase. Net Income from continuing operations in the fourth quarter 2004 (before inclusion of an income tax benefit) was $172,390, or $0.02 per share, compared to $69,331, or $0.01 per share in the fourth quarter of 2003. Including the income tax benefit in the amount of $450,000, or $0.04 per share, basic and diluted income per share was $0.06. \
\
At December 31, 2004 the Company had a backlog from continuing operations of approximately $3.1 million, as compared with a backlog from continuing operations of $1.1 million at December 31, 2003. \
\
Robert P. Bruno, President & C.O.O., stated "Revenues for fiscal 2004 from continuing operations of Microfluidics were up over 16% over last year. The increase was in large part attributable to sales of our M-700 production Microfluidizer(R) processor systems, which increased by 57% above sales of those units in 2003. The M700 Series is our newest product line and has been well received by all of the industries that we serve, but to the largest extent by the pharmaceutical industry where it is used for both pilot and production processing. We experienced significant increases in both the unit sales and revenues from basic and high-end laboratory machines, and increased sales of parts. In addition to providing production units incorporating many of our newer features and options including "Ultra Clean In Place", "Steam In Place" sterilization options, "Product Containment" and "Split System", we also provided solutions and systems that integrated advanced operating controls and instrumentation, and data acquisition capabilities." \
\
Irwin Gruverman, CEO and Chairman stated "Management is pleased with the revenue and income results attained in 2004. The early 2004 sale of the assets of our Morehouse-COWLES Division has, as planned, allowed the Company to focus on the core business and competency of Microfluidics. Our ability to raise capital and restructure our debt and loan facility on a favorable basis in the first quarter of 2004 permitted us to invest in R&D, infrastructure and marketing/sales for Microfluidics in 2004 at a significantly higher rate than we had been able to undertake for several years. This included progress in product development programs for our core Microfluidizer(R) processor systems. Additionally, we have been able to bring forward the commercial development of our new patented Microfluidizer(R) Mixer/Reactor for production of nanoparticles (MMR). The results of the initiatives undertaken in 2004 have been very positive and our business is both in a much improved financial condition and positioned for continued growth. Inquiry levels, quotations and anticipated orders to date in 2005 are strong, while the backlog remains solid. As is common for capital goods manufacturers, the timing of orders can significantly affect the size of quarterly revenues. We have experienced substantial improvements in sales, profitability and cash flow." \
