WASHINGTON - (BUSINESS WIRE) - Feb. 8, 2005 - Nanotech industry leaders from across America arrived in Washington, DC, today to advance a pro-technology agenda under the umbrella of the NanoBusiness Alliance, the leading nanotech policy and commercialization advocate in the United States. The Alliance, comprised of leaders of large and small companies, research institutions, and regional partnerships, will meet with top officials in the Congress and the Administration seeking to secure full federal funding and strong leadership for nanotech initiatives. \
\
The NanoBusiness Alliance's 2005 Public Policy Tour is a two-day session involving meetings with key legislators, members of the Bush administration, and officials in major government agencies such as the Departments of Defense, Energy, and Homeland Security, and the National Institutes of Health. \
\
The Alliance also will co-host a media briefing with the Congressional Nano Caucus, featuring remarks by caucus co-chairs Senators George Allen (R-VA) and Ron Wyden (D-OR) and Rep. Sherwood Boehlert (R-NY). The briefing will take place on Wednesday, February 9, at 9:15 a.m. in Room 106 of the Dirksen Senate Office Building. \
\
"This annual tour is of tremendous importance as nanotechnology increasingly becomes one of the key drivers of US economic growth," said Sean Murdock, Executive Director for the NanoBusiness Alliance. "Our goal this year is to secure full funding for federal nanotech R&D, and advance other policies to solidify America's global leadership at a time when nanotech's economic and quality of life benefits are emerging throughout the world." \
\
Previous Alliance visits to Washington helped win passage of the 21st Century Nanotechnology R&D Act of 2003, which authorizes $3.7 billion in federal funding for nanotech research and development over four years. This year, the Alliance is focusing on ensuring American leadership in the years ahead. Key policy issues beyond R&D funding include a pro-innovation regulatory regime, incentives for commercialization of nanotech breakthroughs, and high priority applications of nanotech in national defense, homeland security, electronics and information technology, life sciences and environmental protection. \
