\
Accelrys Announces Formation of Scientific Advisory Board for Recently Launched Nanotechnology Consortium \
1732 words\
27 January 2005\
21:33 GMT\
Business Wire\
English\
(c) 2005 Business Wire. All Rights Reserved.\
SAN DIEGO - (BUSINESS WIRE) - Jan. 27, 2005 - Accelrys (Nasdaq:ACCL), the leading provider of modeling and simulation software for nanotechnology, today announced the formation of its Nanotechnology Consortium Scientific Advisory Board, consisting of nine well known academic experts in the areas of nanotechnology, chemistry, computational physics and engineering. The company has appointed Professor C. Richard A. Catlow, Dr. Tim Clark, Dr. Alessandro De Vita, Professor Julian Gale, Professor Sharon Glotzer, Professor Yoshiyuki Kawazoe, Professor Mike Payne, Dr. Ruben Perez, and Professor Alan H Windle. The goal of the Nanotechnology Consortium is to accelerate the development of software tools that enable the design of nanomaterials and nanodevices, as well as close the gap between scientists and engineers. \
\
"We are extremely pleased to have the highest caliber leading academics provide the Nanotechnology Consortium with advice regarding scientific methods," said Mark J. Emkjer, president and CEO of Accelrys. "Their insight and unparalleled experience will help to ensure that the Nanotechnology Consortium remains current and relevant with new scientific developments." \
\
"It is truly an honour to have been appointed to the Scientific Advisory Board for the recently launched Accelrys Nanotechnology Consortium," said Professor Mike Payne, Chair of Computational Physics at the University of Cambridge. "The Advisory Board is enthusiastically anticipating its role in fostering the commercial agenda of the Consortium as well, thus significantly accelerating the ability of the membership to develop leading edge, innovative consumer products." \
\
Members of the Scientific Advisory Board are as follows: \
\
Professor C. Richard A. Catlow \
\
Professor Richard Catlow is Wolfson Professor of Natural Philosophy and Director, Davy Faraday Research Laboratory, Royal Institution of Great Britain and Head of Chemistry, University College London. He pioneered the development and application of computer modeling in solid state and materials chemistry. Of particular note is the highly significant and widely quoted work on silicate minerals. In 2004 he was made a Fellow of the Royal Society. \
\
Dr. Tim Clark \
\
Dr. Clark is currently technical Director of the Computer-Chemie-Centrum in Erlangen. His research areas include the development and application of quantum mechanical methods in inorganic, organic and biological chemistry, electron-transfer theory and the simulation of organic and inorganic reaction mechanisms. He holds a BSc. (Hons) from University of Kent at Canterbury, and a PhD from the Queen's University Belfast for work on the thermochemistry and solid phase properties of adamantane and diamantane derivatives. Following an ICI Postdoctoral Fellow in Belfast, and a NATO Postdoctoral Fellowship at Princeton University, he joined the Institut fur Organische Chemie of the Universitat Erlangen-Nurnberg in 1976. He is the author of over 200 articles in scientific journals and two books and is the editor of the Journal of Molecular Modeling. \
\
Dr. Alessandro De Vita \
\
Dr. De Vita graduated in Physics from University of Trieste in 1989, and completed two doctorates, in Solid State Physics and in Materials Engineering. He worked in research appointments with Mike Gillan at Keele, Volker Heine and Mike Payne at Cambridge, and Roberto Car at IRRMA/EPFL Lausanne. He has been Assistant Professor in Materials Engineering at University of Trieste since 1998, and recently joined King's College London as a Reader in Physics. He is the author of over 90 papers. His main interests lie in atomistic quantum mechanics-based algorithms for Materials Science, hybrid classical/quantum techniques, modeling biomaterial interfaces and modeling the self-assembly of supramolecular nanostructures. \
\
Professor Yoshiyuki Kawazoe \
\
Prof. Kawazoe is currently Professor of Materials Design by Computer Simulation, and Director of the Center for Computational Materials Science at the Institute for Materials Research (IMR) in Tohoku University. Additionally, he serves as Advisory Professor of Fudan University, Shanghai, and Senan University, Chongching, China. He graduated with a PhD in Physics from Tohoku University He is the author of a number of papers dealing with materials design via computer simulation, including "Phase Diagrams and Physical Properties of Nonequilibrium Alloys" and "Materials Design by Computer Simulation." \
\
Professor Julian Gale \
\
Professor Julian Gale is the author of the General Utility Lattice Program (GULP) and a co-author of the linear-scaling density functional code SIESTA. He graduated in Chemistry from the University of Oxford, where he subsequently undertook his postgraduate studies under the supervision of Prof. Tony Cheetham in the Department of Chemical Crystallography. After obtaining his DPhil, he moved to the Royal Institution of Great Britain to take up a postdoctoral position jointly with Prof. Richard Catlow and Prof. Bill Mackrodt from ICI Chemical & Polymers. In 1993 he was awarded a Royal Society University Research Fellowship, held at Imperial College London, where he was subsequently appointed as Reader in Theoretical and Computational Chemistry. In 2003 he was awarded one of the two inaugural Premier's Research Fellowships by the Government of Western Australia and moved to become Professor of Computational Chemistry in the Nanochemistry Research Institute at Curtin University of Technology in Perth. \
\
Professor Sharon Glotzer \
\
Prof. Glotzer is Associate Professor of Chemical Engineering, Materials Science and Engineering, Macromolecular Science and Engineering, and Physics at the University of Michigan in Ann Arbor. She graduated with a bachelor's degree in Physics from the University of California, Los Angeles and holds a PhD in Physics from Boston University. Prior to her current position, Prof. Glotzer was Director for the Center for Theoretical and Computational Materials Science (CTCMS), Materials Science and Engineering Laboratory (MSEL), at the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST). She is the author of nearly 100 scientific journal articles and has presented well over 100 invited talks on computational materials science, including nanomaterials design and self-assembly. Her most recent work focuses on the design of nanoparticle "analogues" of molecules, and their bottom-up assembly into novel structures. She serves on the editorial advisory boards of the journals Nanotechnology, AiChE, and Computers in Science and Engineering. She currently serves as vice-chair of the Forum for Industrial and Applied Physics of the American Physical Society, and chair of the Nanoscale Science and Engineering Forum of the American Institute of Chemical Engineers. \
\
Professor Mike Payne \
\
Professor Mike Payne holds the Chair of Computational Physics at the University of Cambridge. He has worked on first principles total energy calculations since 1985 and is the author of the first principles total energy pseudopotential code CASTEP. He was awarded he 1996 Maxwell Medal and Prize by the Institute of Physics and gave the 1998 Mott Lecture. He was the 23rd most highly cited Physical Scientist in the UK between 1990 and 1999 - 2,869 citations (Institute for Scientific Information). He has published over 200 papers. \
\
Dr. Ruben Perez \
\
Dr. Perez graduated in Theoretical Physics at Universidad Complutense de Madrid, and has a Doctorate in Condensed Matter Physics from Universidad Autonoma de Madrid. Following a postdoc with Prof. Mike Payne at Cambridge University, he returned to Madrid, where he holds the position of Associate Professor at Universidad Autonoma. He has been a visiting professor at Osaka University and guest researcher at a number of laboratories including JRCAT (Tsukuba, Japan), MPI-MF (Stuttgart, Germany) and Lawrence Berkeley Lab, UCB. He is the author of more than 70 papers. His research interests include atomistic simulations of mechanical properties of materials, and the modeling of scanning probe microscopes from first principles. \
\
Professor Alan H. Windle \
\
Professor Alan Windle graduated with an Engineering degree in Metallurgy from Imperial College, London and holds a PhD from Cambridge University. He started up the Polymer Group in the Department of Materials Science and Metallurgy at Cambridge in 1975, concentrating initially on the structure of non-crystalline polymers. He is a Fellow of the Royal Society, holds the Bessemer and Royal Society of Arts silver medal from Imperial College, the Rosenhain Medal by the Institute of Metals and the Swinburne Gold Medal and prize by the Plastics and Rubber Institute. Computer modeling has been an important theme in his work over the past 25 years. In particular, he had a strong involvement in starting up Cambridge Molecular Design, one of the predecessors of Accelrys. Carbon Nanotubes have been an important focus of his research in recent years, and a new process for spinning nanotubes into fibers and ribbons has been a recent breakthrough. \
\
About Accelrys \
\
Accelrys, Inc. (NASDAQ:ACCL) is a leading provider of software for computation, simulation, and the management and mining of scientific data used by biologists, chemists and materials scientists, including nanotechnology researchers for product design as well as drug discovery and development. Accelrys technology and services are designed to meet the needs of today's leading research organizations. The company is headquartered in San Diego, California. For more information about Accelrys, visit its website at http://www.accelrys.com/ . \
\
